    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_724d4421622918a4c0cc95eea179b4746577e7c16fd1b17b658e1dce73c42deb",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "For condition (ii), the proof samples the powered function on an equally spaced chain of nearby real parts. Referenced lower-bound inputs make the first shifted mean large, while separate parameter choices ensure that the remaining shifted means do not cancel it cheaply. Their alternating finite difference is then represented by Lemma 8 as an m-fold average of the m-th derivative, forcing derivative mean mass somewhere across the chain. This bridge gains derivative information without termwise differentiation, but its success still depends on the prior lower bounds and the quantitative dominance choices, not on the finite-difference identity alone.",
